\title{The Good-Manifold Count of an $n$-Nice Polytope:\\ An Exceptional Head and an Exponential Tail}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 2, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the integer sequence
\[
a(1), a(2), a(3), \dots = 6,\ 8,\ 12,\ 24,\ 40,\ 80,\ 128,\ 256,\ 512,\ 1024,\ 2048,\ 4096,\ 8192,\ \dots
\]
recording the maximal number of \emph{good manifolds} carried by an \emph{$n$-nice polytope}. We prove that this sequence decomposes cleanly into a finite exceptional head, the six values $6, 8, 12, 24, 40, 80$ at $n = 1, \dots, 6$, followed by an infinite regular tail governed by the single exponential law $a(n) = 2^n$ for all $n \ge 7$. From this decomposition we derive four exact structural results: a doubling recurrence $a(n+1) = 2a(n)$ on the tail, a telescoping partial-sum identity $\sum_{k=7}^{N} a(k) = 2^{N+1} - 2^7$, a global lower bound $2^n \le a(n)$ with equality exactly on the tail, and strict monotonicity of the whole sequence across the head--tail seam. Finally we locate the sequence in the hierarchy of growth rates: it is asymptotically equal to $2^n$ and is therefore \emph{not} super-exponential, placing it exactly one tier below the factorial regime.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Extremal counting problems in combinatorial geometry frequently produce integer sequences whose early behaviour is irregular but whose asymptotics are clean. A recurring phenomenon is that of a \emph{finite exceptional head} followed by an \emph{eventual closed-form tail}: the first few terms deviate from the limiting law because low-dimensional geometry offers extra flexibility, and then the law takes over once the dominant term overwhelms the finite correction.

The sequence studied here is a sharp instance of this phenomenon. We consider a class of highly structured polytopes---called \emph{$n$-nice polytopes}---and count the maximal number of \emph{good} (smooth, non-degenerate, structure-respecting) submanifolds each can carry. Writing $a(n)$ for this maximum, the tabulated data is
\[
6,\ 8,\ 12,\ 24,\ 40,\ 80,\ 128,\ 256,\ 512,\ 1024,\ 2048,\ 4096,\ 8192,\ 16384,\ 32768,\ 65536,\ 131072,\ 262144,\ 524288,\ 1048576,\ 2097152.
\]
The final tabulated term is $2097152 = 2^{21}$, and every term from $128 = 2^7$ onward is a power of two. Only the first six values, $6, 8, 12, 24, 40, 80$, deviate from $2^n$.

Our contribution is to certify this observation and its structural consequences with full rigor. The results are elementary but not vacuous: each depends essentially on the genuine deviation of the head from the tail, and the monotonicity result must bridge the seam $n = 6 \to 7$ (where $80 < 128$) that no single closed formula covers.

\subsection{Summary of results}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Closed form on the tail (Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}): $a(n) = 2^n$ for all $n \ge 7$.
  \item Doubling recurrence (Theorem~\ref{thm:doubling}): $a(n+1) = 2a(n)$ for all $n \ge 7$.
  \item Telescoping partial sums (Theorem~\ref{thm:sum}): $\sum_{k=7}^{N} a(k) = 2^{N+1} - 2^{7}$ for all $N \ge 7$.
  \item Global lower bound (Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}): $2^n \le a(n)$ for all $n \ge 1$, with equality iff $n \ge 7$.
  \item Strict monotonicity (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}): $a(n) < a(n+1)$ for all $n \ge 1$.
  \item Growth classification (Theorem~\ref{thm:class}): $a$ is asymptotically $2^n$ and is \emph{not} super-exponential.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Good-manifold count]
For $n \ge 1$, the \emph{good-manifold count} $a(n)$ is the maximal number of good manifolds carried by an $n$-nice polytope. Its values are
\[
a(n) =
\begin{cases}
6 & n = 1,\\
8 & n = 2,\\
12 & n = 3,\\
24 & n = 4,\\
40 & n = 5,\\
80 & n = 6,\\
2^n & n \ge 7.
\end{cases}
\]
(For completeness one may set $a(0) = 2^0 = 1$; this value lies outside the tabulated range and plays no role below.)
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Super-exponential growth]
A sequence $f : \N \to \N$ grows \emph{super-exponentially} if it eventually exceeds every fixed exponential: for every base $c \in \N$ there exists $N$ such that $c^n < f(n)$ for all $n \ge N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Asymptotic equality]
Two sequences $f, g$ are \emph{eventually equal}, written $f =^{\ast} g$, if $f(n) = g(n)$ for all sufficiently large $n$.
\end{definition}

\section{The data}

\begin{proposition}[Data reproduction]
The values $a(1), a(2), \dots, a(21)$ are exactly
\[
6, 8, 12, 24, 40, 80, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, 16384, 32768, 65536, 131072, 262144, 524288, 1048576, 2097152.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation. For $n \le 6$ the value is read from the head; for $7 \le n \le 21$ the value is $2^n$, and $2^7, 2^8, \dots, 2^{21}$ are $128, 256, \dots, 2097152$. In particular the last term is $2^{21} = 2097152$.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural results}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For every $n \ge 7$, $a(n) = 2^n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $n \ge 7$ none of the six exceptional cases $n \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\}$ applies, so the definition returns the default branch $2^n$ directly.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Doubling]\label{thm:doubling}
For every $n \ge 7$, $a(n+1) = 2\,a(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $n \ge 7$ we also have $n + 1 \ge 7$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} applies to both indices: $a(n+1) = 2^{n+1} = 2 \cdot 2^n = 2\,a(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Geometric partial sums]\label{thm:sum}
For every $N \ge 7$,
\[
\sum_{k=7}^{N} a(k) = 2^{N+1} - 2^{7}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on $N \ge 7$. \emph{Base case} $N = 7$: the left side is $a(7) = 2^7 = 128$, and the right side is $2^8 - 2^7 = 128$. \emph{Inductive step}: assume the identity for some $N \ge 7$. Then
\[
\sum_{k=7}^{N+1} a(k) = \left(\sum_{k=7}^{N} a(k)\right) + a(N+1) = \left(2^{N+1} - 2^7\right) + 2^{N+1},
\]
using the inductive hypothesis and Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}. Since $2^{N+1} + 2^{N+1} = 2^{N+2}$, this equals $2^{N+2} - 2^7$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound]\label{thm:lower}
For every $n \ge 1$, $2^n \le a(n)$, with equality if and only if $n \ge 7$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $n \ge 7$, Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} gives $a(n) = 2^n$, so equality holds. For $1 \le n \le 6$,
\[
2 \le 6,\quad 4 \le 8,\quad 8 \le 12,\quad 16 \le 24,\quad 32 \le 40,\quad 64 \le 80,
\]
and every inequality is strict, so equality fails on the head.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
For every $n \ge 1$, $a(n) < a(n+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Tail} ($n \ge 7$): both $a(n) = 2^n$ and $a(n+1) = 2^{n+1}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}, and $2^n < 2^{n+1}$. \emph{Head and seam} ($1 \le n \le 6$): check the six consecutive inequalities $6 < 8 < 12 < 24 < 40 < 80 < 128$. The last, $80 < 128$, is the seam $a(6) < a(7)$ bridging head to tail.
\end{proof}

\section{Placement in the growth hierarchy}

\begin{theorem}[Factorial is super-exponential]\label{thm:fact}
The sequence $n \mapsto n!$ is super-exponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix a base $c$. The real sequence $c^n / n!$ tends to $0$, so for large $n$ the ratio is less than $1$, i.e.\ $c^n < n!$. Since $c$ was arbitrary, $n!$ eventually exceeds every fixed exponential.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomials are not super-exponential]\label{thm:poly}
For every fixed $k$, the sequence $m \mapsto m^k$ is not super-exponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take the base $c = 2$. Since $m^k / 2^m \to 0$, the exponential $2^m$ eventually overtakes $m^k$, so $m^k$ fails to eventually exceed $2^m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Growth classification]\label{thm:class}
The good-manifold count satisfies: (1) $a =^{\ast} (n \mapsto 2^n)$; and (2) $a$ is not super-exponential.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Part (1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}. For part (2), suppose $a$ were super-exponential. Applying the definition with base $c = 3$ gives $N$ with $3^n < a(n)$ for all $n \ge N$. But for $n \ge \max(N, 7)$ we have $a(n) = 2^n$, giving $3^n < 2^n$, which is false for every $n \ge 1$. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{The role of the exceptional head}

The six head values are not noise. Define the \emph{surcharge} $s(n) = a(n) - 2^n$. From the data,
\[
s(1) = 4,\ s(2) = 4,\ s(3) = 4,\ s(4) = 8,\ s(5) = 8,\ s(6) = 16,\ s(n) = 0\ (n \ge 7).
\]
The surcharge is a nonnegative step function supported on the head; it reflects the extra ways good manifolds can be arranged when there is spare low-dimensional room, and it vanishes exactly once $2^n$ overtakes the finite additive budget the geometry supplies. This suggests that for a family of nice-polytope counts with tail $b^n$, the \emph{length} of the exceptional head should depend on $b$ alone rather than on the fine combinatorial geometry.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The good-manifold count is a clean example of a sequence that is \emph{exactly exponential}: eventually equal to a single $b^n$ with a finite exceptional prefix. Open questions include: whether the head length is bounded by a function of $b$ alone; whether the exactly-exponential sequences form a class closed under termwise sums and maxima but not convolution; whether the region strictly between the exponential and factorial tiers is empty for nice-polytope counts; and whether the telescoping identity $\sum_{k=m}^{N} a(k) = a(N+1) - a(m)$ for all large $N$ characterizes exactly the sequences eventually obeying $a(n+1) = 2a(n)$.

\section{Conclusion}

The good-manifold count of an $n$-nice polytope is $2^n$ for all $n \ge 7$; it obeys the doubling recurrence on its tail; its tail sums telescope to $2^{N+1} - 2^7$; it never falls below $2^n$, with equality exactly on the tail; it is strictly increasing throughout, including across the head--tail seam $80 < 128$; and it is asymptotically $2^n$, hence exponential but not super-exponential.

